cad-chr-vs-loaded-encoding — (chr N) not = to the same char loaded from a file (BricsCAD)
=========================================================================================

Reported from: SCHMS+ test harness via `alfe --bricscad --mode batch`
on macOS. alfe 1.7.3 / clautolisp 1.7.2.

Context / progress
------------------

alfe 1.7.x's per-situation encodings help alfe's OWN file I/O:
- `-Efile Windows-1252` : `read-char` on a file alfe opens returns the
  right code (probe: byte 0xE9 -> 233). Good.

BUT `-Esource` does NOT reach BricsCAD's native `load`. Isolated probe
on a raw cp1252 `.lsp` (byte 0xE9 = "é", no BOM), Mac BricsCAD V26:

    -Esource Windows-1252 -l cp1252.lsp -> (= src-e (chr 233))=nil, ascii=200
    (no -Esource)         -l cp1252.lsp -> (= src-e (chr 233))=nil, ascii=200

Identical: for the `-l`/`load` path, BricsCAD decodes the file itself
via SYSCODEPAGE (=MAC_ROMAN on macOS), so 0xE9 is mis-read as the
Mac-Roman char (ascii 200), and `-Esource` is a no-op. On Mac you
CANNOT native-load a Windows-1252 source as-is.

Remaining bug
-------------

On BricsCAD, a character produced by `(chr N)` for N >= 128 is NOT
`=` (nor `equal`) to the *same* character obtained by decoding a
cp1252 file, even though both have `strlen` 1. So the two internal
representations differ.

Minimal repro
-------------

    printf '(setq src-e "\xe9")\n' > /tmp/se.lsp     # a .lsp holding cp1252 é

    alfe --no-init --bricscad --mode batch \
         -Esource Windows-1252 -Efile Windows-1252 \
         -l /tmp/se.lsp \
         -x '(princ (list (= src-e (chr 233)) (strlen src-e) (strlen (chr 233))))'

    BricsCAD:   (nil 1 1)     ; loaded-é  /=  (chr 233)
    clautolisp: (T   1 1)     ; identical

(Also: `(chr 233)` prints as empty on a UTF-8 terminal but its strlen is
1 — so it's an internal-representation mismatch, not a dropped char.)

Impact
------

Any code that rebuilds strings from char codes and compares them to
strings read from source is broken for accented chars on BricsCAD.
The SCHMS+ test harness reads test files char-by-char
(`tu:read-form`: `read-char` -> `(chr code)` -> `(read text)`) to
transform assertion forms, then compares the resulting literals to
strings from modules loaded via `-Esource`. Result: the 13
`description_validateur/*` accent tests fail on BricsCAD (all logic
passes); clautolisp is 177/177 OK.

Expected
--------

`(= (chr N) <same-char-decoded-from-a-file>)` should hold for all N,
i.e. `chr`/`read`/`strcat` and the file-decode path must yield one
canonical internal representation per character — as clautolisp does.

Why it can't be fixed from AutoLISP on BricsCAD
-----------------------------------------------

Probed on BricsCAD V26 / macOS:
- `(getvar "SYSCODEPAGE")` => "MAC_ROMAN"  and it is READ-ONLY
  (`(setvar "SYSCODEPAGE" "ansi_1252")` errors, value stays MAC_ROMAN).
- `(getvar "DWGCODEPAGE")` => "ANSI_1252"  (per-drawing, not the source
  codepage; also not settable via setvar here).
- `(load file [onfailure])` has no encoding parameter; no AutoLISP
  global controls it.

So BricsCAD's native `chr` uses the fixed system codepage (Mac Roman on
macOS) and there is NO *sysvar* knob to change it.

Concrete fix path — feed BricsCAD a Unicode (UTF-8+BOM) source
--------------------------------------------------------------

Since BricsCAD V20, `load` also accepts a Unicode `.lsp` when it carries
a Byte-Order-Mark: UTF-8+BOM or UTF-16LE+BOM (BOM mandatory; UTF-8
without BOM may fail). Loading such a file bypasses SYSCODEPAGE and makes
`chr` consistent. Verified on BricsCAD V26 / macOS:

Measured on Mac BricsCAD V26, same "é" via native `-l`:

    encoding of file        (= src-e (chr 233))   ascii src-e
    ---------------------   -------------------   -----------
    cp1252 (no BOM)                nil                200
    cp1252 + -Esource              nil                200   (flag ignored)
    UTF-8 + BOM                     T                 233   <-- correct
    MacRoman (é=0x8E)               T                 233   <-- correct

    printf '\xef\xbb\xbf(setq src-e "\xc3\xa9")\n' > /tmp/utf8bom.lsp   # UTF-8+BOM
    printf '(setq src-e "\x8e")\n'                 > /tmp/macroman.lsp  # MacRoman

Both correct encodings give ascii 233 and `(chr 233)` consistency.
(UTF-16LE+BOM via `-l` produced no output — alfe seems to pre-read the
file and not handle UTF-16 input; UTF-8+BOM is the reliable Unicode one.)

MacRoman is a poor target: Mac-only (wrong on Windows where
SYSCODEPAGE=cp1252) and lossy (no €, œ/Œ, Ÿ, ‰). **UTF-8+BOM is
lossless and cross-platform (V20+).**

Suggested alfe behaviour: when handing a file to a CAD host's native
`load`, transcode the `-Esource`-decoded text to a temporary
**UTF-8-with-BOM** copy and load that, on every platform. This fixes the
13 SCHMS+ accent tests with the on-disk sources left untouched (still
cp1252/CRLF for AutoCAD & the rest of the toolchain), and makes
`-Esource` actually effective for `load` (today it is a no-op there).
Alternatively, make `(chr N)` yield the same canonical representation as
the file-decode path.

Related: cad-load-encoding-macos.issue (the encoding-flag part, now
resolved by -Esource/-Efile).
